.TH MINSEC-ID-SYNC 1 "2026-10-26" "minsec-id" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
minsec-id-sync \- share minsec-id finding counts with minsec.io and ask the crowd about modified package files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minsec-id-sync
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minsec-id-sync
is the opt-in crowd client for
.BR minsec-id (1).
It is a short-lived program run hourly by a systemd timer.
It enrolls the host with minsec.io, reports how many findings of each check
are open, and asks whether other hosts report the same content for this
host's modified package files.
.PP
The scanner and the daemon contain no HTTP or TLS client.
This helper reads the scanner's
.I state.json
and writes only its own directory; the crowd's answers reach the scanner as a
file,
.IR crowd.json ,
which the next scan reads.
Creating
.I /etc/minsec-id/sync.toml
is the opt-in switch used by the packaged timer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fIfile\fR ", " \-\-config " " \fIfile\fR
Read configuration from
.I file
instead of
.IR /etc/minsec-id/sync.toml .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the program version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS enroll
Create an Ed25519 signing key if necessary, solve the server's enrollment
proof-of-work challenge, and save the assigned host ID.
The host enrolls as a minsec-id agent: its key can submit findings and hash
checks and nothing else.
With
.BR install_token ,
the host enrolls into the organization that issued it, under its
.BR label .
If this state is already present, print the existing host ID and return,
unless an install token has been added to a host enrolled anonymously: then
the key is moved to
.I key.old
and the host enrolls again, into the organization, once.
If the local host ID was lost but the server already knows the key, the key
is moved to
.I key.old
and enrollment is retried once with a new key.
.SS report
Count the open findings in the scanner's state, per check and severity, and
submit the counts: open, accepted, and new since the previous report.
A report also carries the checks the last scan ran and the distribution
release (os-release
.B ID
and
.BR VERSION_ID ).
It never carries a finding's subject, message, or detail.
Each report is also the host's heartbeat; a host in an organization also
sends its current label.
.SS check
For each open
.B pkg-modified
finding about content, send the package (the rpm NEVRA or the dpkg
.IR name : arch = version ),
the path, the digest algorithm, the digest the package database records, and
the digest of the file on disk; never the file.
minsec.io answers with how many other hosts report the same content for that
package and path, how many report other content, and how many record a
different expected digest in their own package databases, each counted once
per enrollment network.
The verdict is
.B shared
when at least the server's quorum (three by default) of other hosts report
the same content, and
.B rare
otherwise.
.PP
Answers are written to
.IR crowd.json ;
the next scan adds them to the finding's message and to its detail as
.BR crowd .
Severity is never changed by the crowd: content many independent hosts share
was most likely changed by the package's own scripts or a common local
practice, but a crowd that could lower severities would be worth poisoning.
Each finding is asked about again daily while it stays open, which also keeps
this host's vote on the server.
.PP
rpm packages no key signed are not sent (their names are often local) unless
.B unsigned
is set, and packages matching
.B exclude
are never sent.
.SS run
Perform the timer workflow: enroll if necessary, then
.B report
and
.B check
according to configuration.
If the configuration file does not exist, exit successfully without output or
side effects.
This absent-file behavior is specific to
.BR run ;
the other commands report an error.
.SS status
Print the configured server, public key, host ID, organization, next
report sequence number, and how many crowd answers are on file.
A key is generated if one does not already exist.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/minsec-id/sync.toml
is TOML; every key is optional and unknown keys are an error.
.TP
.BR server " (default " \(dqhttps://api.minsec.io\(dq )
The backend's base URL.
.TP
.BR state_dir " (default " \(dq/var/lib/minsec-id\(dq )
The scanner's state directory.
Its
.I state.json
is read; its
.I sync
directory holds this client's files.
.TP
.BR findings " (default " true )
Report finding counts.
.TP
.BR hashcheck " (default " true )
Ask the crowd about modified package files.
.TP
.BR unsigned " (default " false )
Include rpm packages no key signed in hash checks.
.TP
.BR exclude " (default " [] )
Package ids never sent in a hash check: globs where
.B *
matches anything, such as
.BR \(dqacme-*\(dq .
.TP
.BR install_token " (default none)"
An organization's install token, for fleet management on minsec.io.
The host's reports then appear in the organization's fleet views, a host
that stops reporting raises a missed-heartbeat alert to the organization,
and its hash checks count once per organization, not once per network.
The token is a secret: keep the file readable by root only.
.TP
.BR label " (default the hostname)"
The host's name in its organization's fleet views and alerts: letters,
digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR - ,
at most 64 characters; other characters are dropped.
.B \(dq\(dq
sends none.
Sent only by a host in, or joining, an organization.
.SH FLEETS
An organization is a minsec.io customer's set of hosts.
Its install tokens are minted by minsec.io; each may expire or be limited to
a number of hosts.
A host enrolled with one is bound to the organization for the life of its
enrollment, and is the only kind of host that sends a label.
Hosts enrolled without a token are anonymous: nothing links them to each
other or to an owner.
.PP
A host that has not reported for the organization's heartbeat timeout
(three hours by default; reports are hourly) is reported to the
organization by webhook or mail, and so is its next report.
On a compromised host, an agent that stops reporting is what an intruder
silencing it looks like.
A decommissioned host is retired by minsec.io so it stops being watched.
.SH OPERATION
The packaged
.B minsec-id-sync.timer
runs
.B minsec-id-sync run
about once an hour: five minutes after boot, then an hour after each run,
with up to ten minutes of randomized delay.
The service runs as root with no capabilities, so it can read the scanner's
root-only state file as its owner but nothing past file permissions, and
writes only
.IR /var/lib/minsec-id/sync .
It needs HTTPS access to the server and a CA trust store.
.PP
To opt out, remove
.IR /etc/minsec-id/sync.toml .
Existing crowd answers stay in
.I crowd.json
until removed; delete it to drop them from findings.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minsec-id/sync.toml
Configuration; its existence is the opt-in.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/minsec-id/sync.toml.example
An annotated example.
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec-id/sync/key
The Ed25519 identity key (32-byte seed, mode 0600).
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec-id/sync/state.json
Host ID, report sequence, and when the last report was accepted.
.TP
.I /var/lib/minsec-id/sync/crowd.json
The crowd's answers, by finding fingerprint, read by the scanner.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR minsec-id (1),
.BR minsec-id.toml (5),
https://minsec.io/privacy
